  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A Noreaster will impact at least Downeast Maine\nthis weekend into Monday, bringing gusty winds, high surf, and \nperiods of rain.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nWe are still expecting a strong Noreaster impacting our area\nfrom today through the weekend and into Monday. However, we \nwill be on the far northern fringe of the Noreaster with the \ncenter staying south of Massachusetts through Sunday, and \nimpacts this far north through Sunday will be much less than \nareas further south. Here is what to expect for the various \ncomponents of the storm:\n\nPeriods of Rain...Rain chances will be far greater Downeast than\nnorthern areas for the storm, with rain developing on Saturday,\nand persisting off and on through Monday. There is still \nsubstantial uncertainty on the northern extent of the rain. Most\nlikely Downeast up to Bangor will see periods of rain through \nthe weekend, but totals could be anywhere from one quarter inch \nto one and a half inches, due to the uncertainty of how far \nnorth the rain will extent, as there is still a lot of \nuncertainty. Even with the higher totals, we would not expect \nflooding. Sunday night and Monday, a much weaker version of the \nNoreaster finally lifts north and tracks into the Gulf of Maine,\nat which point rain chances finally increase over the whole \narea. That said, not expecting anything heavy.\n\nGusty Winds...Winds from the northeast will increase Friday\nnight into Saturday, remain gusty through Sunday, then decrease\na bit into Monday. Winds will be stronger the further south you\ngo, with gusts topping out around only 20 mph in Northern Maine\nthis weekend, but 25-35 mph Bangor and Downeast. We don't\nanticipate impacts from the wind other than maybe a very\nisolated outage Downeast.\n\nHigh Surf...Seas near the shore will be fairly rough with 5-9 ft\nseas, much of it as a longer period south/southeast swell. We\nare not expecting impactful coastal flooding, though waves\nbreaking on the rocks will be hazardous for any onlookers.\nConditions should be a bit more hazardous for coastal Hancock\nthan coastal Washington. Conditions slowly ease Sunday night \ninto Monday.",
